  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The algorithm's performance and safety benefits are demonstrated solely through simulated scenarios rather than real-world physical deployments.",
      "what": "The Dynamic Real-Time Trajectory Smoothing Algorithm (DRTSA) was developed to optimize vehicle arrival times and speeds at traffic bottlenecks, generating piecewise optimal accelerations as advisory controls. The framework was evaluated via simulations of two bottleneck scenarios—a slow-moving vehicle and a signalized intersection—across varying traffic densities, connected automated vehicle (CAV) penetration rates, and control ranges.",
      "why": "It provides a single, generalizable trajectory optimization framework capable of mitigating both recurrent and non-recurrent bottlenecks under mixed-autonomy traffic conditions, improving throughput and reducing speed oscillations simultaneously."
    }
  },
